# Zip Codes Page — Import/Export Fixes & Enhancements

## Context
Three fixes + one new feature for `app/routes/app.zip-codes.tsx`:
1. Export button fires multiple downloads because the download logic runs in the render body (not a `useEffect`), so every React re-render while `fetcher.data` holds the CSV triggers another download.
2. No "Download Sample CSV" template in the import modal — competitors universally provide this.
3. The bulk-import CSV parser splits naively on every comma, breaking fields like `"Manhattan, NY"` that contain commas inside quotes. This causes a silent data-corruption bug on export→reimport round-trips since the export wraps all fields in double-quotes.

## Critical File
`app/routes/app.zip-codes.tsx` — all changes are in this single file.

---

## Change 1 — Fix Export Multi-Download Bug

**Root cause:** Lines 545–553 run directly in the render body. Every re-render (React Strict Mode double-invoke, state updates during loading, etc.) re-fires the Blob download while `fetcher.data.action === "export"` is still truthy.

**Fix:** Move the download side-effect into a `useEffect`. Use a `useRef` keyed on the CSV string so the download fires exactly once per unique export result.

```tsx
// add near other refs (top of component)
const exportTriggeredRef = useRef<string | null>(null);

// replace the inline if (exportCsv) { ... } block with:
useEffect(() => {
  if (!exportCsv || exportTriggeredRef.current === exportCsv) return;
  exportTriggeredRef.current = exportCsv;
  const blob = new Blob([exportCsv], { type: "text/csv" });
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  const a = document.createElement("a");
  a.href = url;
  a.download = "zip-codes.csv";
  a.click();
  URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
}, [exportCsv]);
```

---

## Change 2 — Download Sample CSV Template in Import Modal

**What to add:** A plain `Button` with `DownloadIcon` placed between the format-example `Box` (line 1325) and the `DropZone` (line 1327). Clicking it generates a sample CSV in the browser and triggers an immediate download — no server round-trip.

**Sample CSV content** (matches the bulk-import parser's column order):
```
Zip Code,Zone,Status,Message,ETA
10001,Manhattan,allowed,We deliver here! Estimated 2-3 days.,2-3 days
90210,Beverly Hills,allowed,Same day delivery available.,1 day
33101,Miami,blocked,Sorry we do not deliver to this area.,
```

**Implementation:**
```tsx
// Pure client-side helper — no useCallback needed (no deps)
function downloadSampleCsv() {
  const sample = [
    "Zip Code,Zone,Status,Message,ETA",
    "10001,Manhattan,allowed,We deliver here! Estimated 2-3 days.,2-3 days",
    "90210,Beverly Hills,allowed,Same day delivery available.,1 day",
    "33101,Miami,blocked,Sorry we do not deliver to this area.,",
  ].join("\n");
  const blob = new Blob([sample], { type: "text/csv" });
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  const a = document.createElement("a");
  a.href = url;
  a.download = "sample-zip-codes.csv";
  a.click();
  URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
}
```

**UI placement** — after the format-example Box, before the DropZone:
```tsx
<InlineStack align="end">
  <Button variant="plain" icon={DownloadIcon} onClick={downloadSampleCsv}>
    Download sample CSV template
  </Button>
</InlineStack>
```

Import `DownloadIcon` from `@shopify/polaris-icons` (already installed).

---

## Change 3 — Fix CSV Parser for Quoted Fields

**Root cause:** `bulk-import` action (lines ~220–235) splits each row with `.split(",")`. Exported CSV wraps every field in double-quotes (e.g. `"Manhattan, NY"`), so a zone with a comma splits into extra columns and corrupts the parse silently.

**Fix:** Replace the naive split with a small RFC-4180-compatible parser inline:

```typescript
function parseCsvRow(line: string): string[] {
  const cols: string[] = [];
  let cur = "";
  let inQuotes = false;
  for (let i = 0; i < line.length; i++) {
    const ch = line[i];
    if (ch === '"') {
      if (inQuotes && line[i + 1] === '"') { cur += '"'; i++; } // escaped quote
      else { inQuotes = !inQuotes; }
    } else if (ch === "," && !inQuotes) {
      cols.push(cur.trim()); cur = "";
    } else {
      cur += ch;
    }
  }
  cols.push(cur.trim());
  return cols;
}
```

Replace `.split(",").map((c) => c.trim().replace(/^"|"$/g, ""))` with `parseCsvRow(line)` in the `bulk-import` action. The function goes at module scope (top of file, outside component/action).

---

## Verification
1. **Export:** Click Export — file downloads exactly once. Click again — downloads once again (new ref value). No duplicate downloads.
2. **Sample CSV:** Open Import modal → click "Download sample CSV template" → `sample-zip-codes.csv` downloads. Open it in Excel/Sheets — 3 data rows, correct columns.
3. **Import round-trip:** Export existing zip codes → import the exported file → zones with commas (e.g. `"Manhattan, NY"`) parse correctly with no column shifts.
4. Run `npm run typecheck` — zero errors.
5. Run `npm run lint` — zero errors.

